description:
Lesson 5.01's server binds to loopback on a system-chosen port and publishes it in a file. Write both sides of that file, and the checks a client makes:

```cpp
struct Endpoint { std::string host; std::uint16_t port; };
struct PortFile { std::uint16_t port; std::uint32_t pid; std::string token; };

bool isLoopback(const std::string& host);
std::optional<Endpoint> parseEndpoint(const std::string& text);
std::string writePortFile(const PortFile& f);
std::optional<PortFile> readPortFile(const std::string& text);
```

- **isLoopback**: `localhost` (any case), `::1`, or an IPv4 address `127.a.b.c` with each part 0 to 255.
- **parseEndpoint** reads `host:port` or `[::1]:port` (IPv6 in brackets). The port is 1 to 65535, digits only. The host must be loopback; anything else gives `std::nullopt`.
- **writePortFile** writes three lines: `port=50123`, `pid=4120`, `token=...`, each ending in `\n`.
- **readPortFile** reads them back in any order, ignoring blank lines and `\r`; all three keys must appear exactly once, the port must be valid, the pid a number, and the token 32 lowercase hex characters.

